00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
the arrivedcan app was meant to be a  short-term measure forcing everyone who  entered canada during the pandemic to  follow the government's strict  quarantine rules but a recent software  update has broadened the app's reach  passengers arriving at toronto pearson  and vancouver international airports can  now use arrive can to complete customs  declaration forms before landing in  canada other airports are due to follow  in coming months the government says it  saves time for travelers but some tech  analysts suggest the government is  pulling a fast one the idea that that  public health rationale was used  in a time of fear and crisis to push  this forward you can't you can't just  coast off of that and then switch the  purpose of the app critics already  disliked the app contracts were awarded  without competition to unidentify  companies that were already doing  business with the government we need to  know what decisions were made you know  who's you know so who are the suppliers  what are they being paid  why are they being  chosen even if we're using the same ones  what was the criteria that was used in  order to hire them in the first place  canada border services says it spent  almost 25 million dollars developing and  maintaining the app and another 2.2  million dollars on advertising the  government says roughly 700 000  in-person quarantine inspections have  been completed thanks to the app's data  those inspections represent about 14  percent of the total 4.7 million  travelers forced to quarantine after  entering canada  the government insists arrive can's new  function is voluntary and that it  engages the privacy commissioner on any  collection of personal information  but with suspicion already surrounding  the app some say the latest development  is too much it should be decommissioned  full stop this should be shut down so  where does the app go from here a  statement from public safety notes no  decision has been made on the long-term  future of the app but border services  says arrive can will continue to be  available after travel and public health  measures are no longer needed neither  transport minister omar algabra nor  public safety minister marco mendicino  was available for comment  ross lord global news
 

